{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B011",
        "laureates": [
          "Bertrand I. Halperin",
          "David R. Nelson",
          "A. Peter Young"
        ],
        "citation": "for the theory of defect-mediated two-dimensional melting and the prediction of the hexatic phase"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B043",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B011 isolates a simple, universal physical mechanism: topological-defect unbinding accounts for melting in two dimensions and entails the intervening hexatic phase. This is not only an elegant construction; its distinctive consequences have been scrutinized over a long period in theoretical, experimental, and soft-matter settings. Halperin, Nelson, and Young provide a coherent three-person representation of the central framework, with a more naturally bounded physical citation than broader platform or method proposals.",
    "case_against": "The idealized transition is not realized identically in every two-dimensional material or film, so the committee must not overstate the uniformity of empirical confirmation. The division of credit among the original KTHNY contributions and later elaborations also requires final documentary scrutiny. These reservations are substantial, but they concern empirical scope and exact historical allocation rather than the existence of the central mechanism.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson describes B043 as a durable, nonperturbative and experimentally relevant framework for correlated materials, while questioning whether its broad development can be credited cleanly to three people or framed as more than a computational method.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B043 as the leading alternative. Its sustained explanatory power is exceptional, but the concern is intrinsic to its prize framing: the foundational construction, later extensions, and realistic-material implementations form a broad continuum. By contrast, B011 identifies a particular physical mechanism, a characteristic phase sequence, and a comparatively compact historical framework. That sharper boundary leaves B011 ahead for me."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that B013 is an independently reproduced 10^-18 precision capability with a particularly clear division between Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's decisive realizations, while recognizing that applications beyond metrology are still developing.",
        "response": "The clarity and maturity of B013 are powerful arguments, and the concise Katori-Ye attribution is a genuine advantage. Yet the award would most securely rest on an extraordinary measurement architecture whose broader fundamental discoveries remain prospective. B011 already changed the physical account of a class of phase transitions through a mechanism with established, discriminating phenomenology; I therefore retain it as my preference rather than elevate attribution simplicity over that explanatory achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson maintains that inflation and primordial quantum perturbations connect early-universe theory to observed cosmic structure, while accepting that observations do not select a unique mechanism and that the three-person lineage needs close review.",
        "response": "I agree that B003 is a profound framework with an important empirical legacy. Its unresolved mechanism and diffuse theoretical lineage, however, make the exact discovery boundary less settled. B011 has a more definite mechanism-to-phenomenology relation and a more delimited set of central contributors, which is decisive in this final comparison."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the experimental record across distinct two-dimensional systems supports a sufficiently unified citation without implying that all real systems realize the ideal transition in the same way.",
      "Whether Halperin, Nelson, and Young are the most historically complete three-person representation of the original defect-mediated-melting framework."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No recusal-requiring connection is apparent from the supplied profile. The shared Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson remains an institutional connection only; no specific collaboration is identified."
  }
}
